 "amendments": {
  "items": {
   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the civil practice law and rules, in relation to interest upon\njudgment",
    "basePrintNo": "S473",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S473-2021",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §5003, CPLR",
    "lawSection": "Civil Practice Law and Rules",
    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S473\n\nSPONSOR: HOYLMAN\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the civil practice law and rules, in relation to inter-\nest upon judgment\n\n \nPURPOSE OF THE BILL:\n\nTo allow for calculation of interest from the original date of entry of\ndenial of summary judgment in cases where such denial is reversed on\nappeal.   \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection one amends the CPLR to require calculation of interest in cases\nwhere summary judgment was first denied, then granted on appeal, from\nthe date of entry of the order originally denying summary judgment.\n\nSection two is the effective date.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nThis legislation would amend the CPLR to require interest on judgments\nto be measured from the date of entry of the order originally denying\nsummary judgment in cases where summary judgment is granted on appeal\nafter being denied. Calculating interest from such a date will result in\na more equitable outcome for those who bring an action that is ultimate-\nly decided in their favor, often after waiting years for resolution of\nthe matter.\n\nIf a plaintiff is granted summary judgment on appeal, it is a matter of\nfairness to allow such plaintiff to receive interest on a judgment from\nthe date of the original denial of summary judgment, because plaintiff\nshould have been granted summary judgment from such date to being with.\nFurthermore, this proposal would help to increase efficiency in the\njudicial system and foster resolution of cases pending appeal where one\nside anticipates the realistic outcome of the case and appeals to extend\nthe litigation and delay payment of a judgment.\n\n \nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nS.6204 of 2019-2020 (Holyman): Died in Judiciary\nA.8394 of 2019-2020 (Rozic): Died in Codes\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS FOR STATE:\n\nNone.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect immediately.\n",
